    "language": [],
    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Arsenic is a naturally occurring groundwater contaminant that has serious and well-documented toxicological effects in humans. During severe droughts, excess groundwater pumping can cause land subsidence and subsurface changes in aquifer conditions that facilitate arsenic release to groundwater. In the San Luis Valley, Colorado, USA, previous work estimated that 30% of the population was exposed to drinking water arsenic in excess of the 10 µg/L maximum contaminant level (MCL) established by the EPA. However, the specific drivers of arsenic release in the groundwater system are not yet known. Through a community engaged sampling effort, we tested arsenic concentrations in 652 water samples from domestic wells across the San Luis Valley and surrounding communities. Approximately 8.9% of wells tested had levels of arsenic in exceedance of the 10 µg/L MCL, and a further 6.7% of wells had arsenic between 5 and 10 µg/L. In conjunction with statistical analysis of these groundwater data, we used ordinary kriging to generate spatial interpolation models of regional arsenic concentrations. The performance of the regional kriging models was evaluated using cross validation and root-mean-squared error on held out testing data. These analyses capture the current extent of elevated arsenic risk across the San Luis Valley and assess the accuracy of spatial predictions of arsenic occurrence where no samples were taken.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
